  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Evaluating Convolution Piece by Piece",
    "lesson_core_move": "Evaluate the convolution integral separately on each time region where the overlap bounds change, then assemble the piecewise output.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Evaluating Convolution Piece by Piece",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Evaluating Convolution Piece by Piece",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the previous convolution steps, the overlap intervals for $x(\\tau)$ and $h(t-\\tau)$ were already found. This lesson starts after those bounds are known. The new move is to evaluate the convolution integral separately on each time region where the overlap bounds have a fixed formula.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On one time region, the overlap gives a lower bound and an upper bound for $\\tau$. Those bounds replace $-\\infty$ and $\\infty$ in the convolution integral. The variable of integration is $\\tau$, while $t$ is treated as a fixed parameter during that regional calculation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_i(t)=\\int_{L_i(t)}^{U_i(t)}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau,\\qquad t\\text{ in region }i",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For each region, use the same workflow: choose the current time region, insert its bounds $L_i(t)$ and $U_i(t)$, integrate with respect to $\\tau$, simplify the result as a formula in $t$, and keep the region condition attached to that formula.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The final output is not one formula produced from one set of bounds. It is a piecewise function assembled from the regional formulas, listed in the same order as the time regions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}y_1(t),&t\\in R_1\\\\y_2(t),&t\\in R_2\\\\y_3(t),&t\\in R_3\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Evaluating Convolution Piece by Piece",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the First Overlap Region",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $0 \\le t \\le 1$, evaluate the first-region convolution integral $y(t)=\\int_0^t 1\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The current time region is $0 \\le t \\le 1$. On this region, the overlap bounds have already been found: $0 \\le \\tau \\le t$. The task is only to evaluate the regional integral and keep the time condition attached.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_0^t 1\\,d\\tau,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The integration variable is $\\tau$, so $t$ is treated as fixed while we take the antiderivative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int 1\\,d\\tau=\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the upper bound $t$ and the lower bound $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\left[\\tau\\right]_0^t=t-0=t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the first overlap region contributes the regional formula $y(t)=t$ for $0 \\le t \\le 1$. This is the first piece, not the full piecewise output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Evaluating Convolution Piece by Piece",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Evaluating a Middle Region with New Bounds",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $1 \\le t \\le 2$, evaluate the middle-region convolution integral $y(t)=\\int_0^1 1\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The current time region is $1 \\le t \\le 2$. The cue is that this is a middle region, so the bounds are not inherited from the first region. Here the overlap bounds are fixed: $0 \\le \\tau \\le 1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_0^1 1\\,d\\tau,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The integration variable is still $\\tau$. Since the integrand is constant and the bounds are fixed, this regional value will be constant on the whole interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int 1\\,d\\tau=\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the fixed upper bound $1$ and lower bound $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\left[\\tau\\right]_0^1=1-0=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the middle overlap region contributes the regional value $y(t)=1$ for $1 \\le t \\le 2$. The method stayed the same; the bounds changed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Evaluating Convolution Piece by Piece",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Last Overlap Region",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $2 \\le t \\le 3$, evaluate the last-region convolution integral $y(t)=\\int_{t-2}^1 1\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The current time region is $2 \\le t \\le 3$. The cue is that this is the last nonzero overlap region, so the overlap is shrinking. The lower bound now depends on $t$: $t-2 \\le \\tau \\le 1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{t-2}^1 1\\,d\\tau,\\qquad 2\\le t\\le 3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The integration variable is still $\\tau$, so $t$ is treated as fixed while we take the antiderivative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int 1\\,d\\tau=\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute upper minus lower carefully. The upper bound is $1$, and the lower bound is $t-2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\left[\\tau\\right]_{t-2}^1=1-(t-2)=3-t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the last overlap region contributes the regional formula $y(t)=3-t$ for $2 \\le t \\le 3$. The result decreases because the lower bound moves upward as $t$ increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Evaluating Convolution Piece by Piece",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Assembling the Piecewise Convolution Output",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $y(t)=t$ for $0 \\le t \\le 1$, $y(t)=1$ for $1 \\le t \\le 2$, and $y(t)=3-t$ for $2 \\le t \\le 3$, assemble the nonzero piecewise convolution output.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the regional formulas have already been evaluated. The task is not to integrate again; it is to preserve each formula with its own time interval and list the pieces in increasing order of $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}y(t)&=t,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1\\\\y(t)&=1,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 2\\\\y(t)&=3-t,\\qquad 2\\le t\\le 3\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now place those three formula-interval pairs under one piecewise definition for $y(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}t,&0\\le t\\le 1\\\\1,&1\\le t\\le 2\\\\3-t,&2\\le t\\le 3\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the nonzero piecewise convolution output. The formulas stay attached to their original intervals, and no outside zero-output regions are added because the prompt asks only for the nonzero overlap pieces.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
